---
import MainLayout from "../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Projects | Haotian Sun" description="Research and technical projects by Haotian Sun.">
  <header class="page-header">
    <p class="eyebrow">Projects</p>
    <h1>Selected work</h1>
    <p class="lead">Research in radio transients, survey calibration, and selection effects.</p>
  </header>

  <section class="project-list">
    <article class="project">
      <div class="project-meta">
        <span>Completed</span>
        <span>MSc thesis</span>
      </div>
      <div class="project-body">
        <p class="eyebrow">CHIME/Slow</p>
        <h2>Characterizing the Selection Function of the Search for Long-Duration Radio Transients with CHIME/Slow</h2>
        <p>
          CHIME/Slow extends CHIME's temporal sensitivity to radio transients with widths from
          approximately 16 milliseconds to 5 seconds. I measured its end-to-end selection function
          using synthetic bursts injected into CHIME intensity data and processed through the complete search pipeline.
        </p>
        <div class="project-facts" aria-label="Thesis overview">
          <div><strong>279,593</strong><span>injections analysed</span></div>
          <div><strong>1,442</strong><span>secure recoveries</span></div>
          <div><strong>16 ms–5 s</strong><span>target width range</span></div>
        </div>

        <h3>What I found</h3>
        <ul class="findings">
          <li><strong>Fluence–width dependence.</strong> Beam-modulated fluence and broadened pulse width organize the dominant recovery structure. The empirical sensitivity boundary is approximately constant below the 16-ms sampling scale and rises with width for broader signals.</li>
          <li><strong>Bright bandwidth.</strong> At comparable beam-modulated fluence and broadened width, signals that remain bright across a larger fraction of the observing band are recovered more frequently. Bright bandwidth captures this spectral-visibility dependence beyond total fluence alone.</li>
          <li><strong>Dispersion measure (DM).</strong> A residual dependence on DM remains after approximately accounting for the primary fluence–width sensitivity. The analysis does not establish DM itself as the direct cause of this trend.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="methodology">
          <p class="eyebrow">Methodological contribution</p>
          <h3>Not all limitations are the same</h3>
          <p class="method-intro">
            A central conclusion of the thesis is that three conceptually distinct limitations of
            injection-derived selection functions are often conflated. Each enters at a different
            stage of the inference problem and requires a different remedy.
          </p>

          <div class="limits-grid">
            <article>
              <span>01</span>
              <h4>Population conditioning</h4>
              <p>
                A low-dimensional completeness map averages over omitted—but represented—variables
                according to their distribution in the injected population. More injections reduce
                statistical noise, but do not remove this dependence. Higher-dimensional modelling or
                reweighting can help when the relevant variables and sufficient support are available.
              </p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <span>02</span>
              <h4>Simulator misspecification</h4>
              <p>
                Morphology absent from the injected signal family—such as multi-component structure,
                sub-burst drift, irregular spectral occupancy, or scintillation—cannot be recovered by
                rebinning, adding more injections from the same simulator, or using a more flexible inference method.
              </p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <span>03</span>
              <h4>Recovery identity</h4>
              <p>
                When injection identity is reconstructed from final candidate parameters, the measured
                recovery function includes matching and verification as well as the physical pipeline response.
                Propagating an injection ID from the earliest practical event stage would isolate these effects.
              </p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
        <ul class="tags" aria-label="Project topics">
          <li>Radio transients</li><li>Injection–recovery</li><li>Selection functions</li><li>Simulator support</li><li>CHIME</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </article>
  </section>

  <aside class="project-note">
    <p>More projects will be added as they develop. In the meantime, visit <a href="/notes">Notes</a> for technical derivations and shorter pieces.</p>
  </aside>
</MainLayout>

<style>
  .page-header { padding-bottom: 52px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .project-list { margin-top: 62px; }
  .project { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 150px 1fr; gap: clamp(28px, 5vw, 64px); }
  .project-meta { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 4px; padding-top: 4px; color: var(--text-soft); font-size: .82rem; }
  .project-meta span:first-child { color: var(--accent-dark); font-weight: 600; }
  .project-body { padding-left: clamp(24px, 4vw, 42px); border-left: 2px solid var(--accent); }
  .project-body h2 { margin: 4px 0 22px; padding: 0; border: 0; font-size: clamp(1.65rem, 3.6vw, 2.7rem); }
  .project-body > p:not(.eyebrow) { max-width: 650px; }
  .project-body h3 { margin-top: 38px; }
  .project-facts { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 1px; margin: 34px 0 10px; background: var(--border); border: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .project-facts div { padding: 18px; background: var(--soft); }
  .project-facts strong { display: block; color: var(--text-strong); font-family: "Space Grotesk", sans-serif; font-size: 1.18rem; }
  .project-facts span { display: block; margin-top: 3px; color: var(--text-soft); font-size: .76rem; }
  .findings { max-width: 680px; margin: 0; padding-left: 1.1rem; }
  .findings li { margin: 0 0 12px; padding-left: .35rem; }
  .methodology { margin-top: 52px; padding-top: 42px; border-top: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .methodology > h3 { margin: 6px 0 12px; font-size: clamp(1.45rem, 2.7vw, 2rem); }
  .method-intro { max-width: 680px; }
  .limits-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 16px; margin-top: 30px; }
  .limits-grid article { padding: 22px 24px 24px; border: 1px solid var(--border); background: var(--soft); }
  .limits-grid span { color: var(--accent-dark); font: 500 .72rem "Space Grotesk", sans-serif; letter-spacing: .12em; }
  .limits-grid h4 { margin: 8px 0 8px; color: var(--text-strong); font: 600 1.08rem "Space Grotesk", sans-serif; }
  .limits-grid p { margin: 0; font-size: .9rem; line-height: 1.68; }
  .tags { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; margin: 28px 0 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; }
  .tags li { margin: 0; padding: 5px 11px; border: 1px solid var(--border); color: var(--text-soft); font-size: .78rem; }
  .project-note { margin-top: 82px; padding: 24px 28px; background: var(--soft); font-size: .94rem; }
  .project-note p { margin: 0; }
  @media (max-width: 650px) { .project { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 20px; } .project-meta { flex-direction: row; gap: 14px; } .project-facts { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
</style>
